  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nUpper ridging presently centered over the northeastern Gulf Coast\nwill build over the Deep South through the end of the week, \nresulting in increasing subsidence and lower rain chances over the\nnext few days. A few diurnal showers and storms still cannot be \nruled out each day, but coverage will be lesser compared to the \npast few days. However, a muggy airmass will persist, and combined\nwith increasing heat, it will result in elevated heat stress \nconcerns through the week. For today, a Heat Advisory remains in \neffect generally west of I-55, where heat indices are more likely \nto persist in the 105-110F range over a longer duration of the \ndaytime. Of course, it will still be uncomfortably warm and humid\neven outside of the advisory. \n\nBy the end of the week into early next week, daytime high temps will \napproach the triple digits at many sites, and several warm minimum \ntemp records in the upper 70s to near 80 will be threatened. With\nheat indices still into the triple digits, additional heat \nadvisories are almost certain. There is at least some hope by the \nend of the week into early next week for increased mixing to \nbring dewpoints down into the 60s during the daytime periods. \nWhile this may not be enough to get us out of the heat stress \ndanger zone, it could at least keep us out of the significant \nheat stress category. Otherwise, an influx of greater deep layer \nmoisture early next week will yield a return to daily isolated to \nscattered diurnal convection across the area. /DL/",
